{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R"] [17592186546796 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R"] [17592186546958 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+y=0 \\\\ -6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(-3x+y)=-6x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186546987 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fde17e6b52cb472d517caee4fcb84e9aee9465ebd391f482316b487486ebe6e0.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/5d/5d95c4d8ab87366960b99dbbcd898214e2ee883da7ceb1926d44968c204d2497.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png)"] [17592186547001 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/033fb4b6f35c06e8d718df9b8e88d47b7461f247121f1e859c6fba41cfda9112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n$y=-2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/ea/eabc6882089c4e26e497b8adae004018e132874dd9e02d33caeddd3967330c87.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/86/868becabde44fa1173fcf0a41424bfcad678a03f5449f62706a7f814563347dc.png)"] [17592186547037 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$"] [17592186547037 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )+15) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"] [17592186547042 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x+6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n3(x+2y)=3x+6y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,x=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2k \\\\ -k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186547083 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 0 & 2 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 